        "role": "user",
        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which sports team does Mario Haas represent or represent?\n\nAnswer A: RC Strasbourg\n  about: Mario Haas\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: 2007\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"In the summer of 2007, Mario Haas made the move to RC Strasbourg, signing a two-year contract with the club.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: professional footballer\n  about: Mario Haas\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"As he approached the outskirts of the village, Mario noticed a group of cyclists clad in sleek, navy-blue jerseys with the Uniqa logo emblazoned on their backs. They were a familiar sight, and he couldn't help but feel a pang of nostalgia. He had often watched them train on these very roads, marveling at their endurance and precision.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Title: The Unlikely Journey of Mario Haas: From Austria to the Heart of France\n\nIn the picturesque city of Graz, Austria, a young Mario Haas was born with a passion for football that would take him on an extraordinary journey. Little did he know that his talent and dedication would one day lead him to don the jersey of RC Strasbourg, a renowned association football club in France. Growing up, Mario's love for the beautiful game was evident in every aspect of his life. He spent hours honing his skills on the pitch, watching matches, and studying the techniques of his idols. His hard work paid off when he began his professional career with Sturm Graz, a top-tier Austrian club, in 1993. Over the next decade, Mario established himself as a prolific striker, earning recognition and accolades in the Austrian Bundesliga. However, Mario's ambition and desire for new challenges soon led him to explore opportunities beyond Austria's borders. In 2005, he made the bold decision to join JEF United Ichihara, a Japanese club, where he spent two seasons adapting to a new style of play and culture. This experience not only broadened his horizons but also refined his skills, making him an attractive prospect for European clubs. It was during this period that RC Strasbourg, a historic French club with a rich legacy, began to take notice of Mario's impressive performances. Founded in 1906, RC Strasbourg had a proud tradition of nurturing talented players and competing at the highest level. The club's scouts were impressed by Mario's versatility, goal-scoring ability, and experience in diverse environments. In the summer of 2007, Mario Haas made the move to RC Strasbourg, signing a two-year contract with the club. This marked a significant milestone in his career, as he became the first Austrian player to join the Alsatian outfit. The Strasbourg faithful welcomed Mario with open arms, eager to see him bring his attacking flair to the Stade de la Meinau. Mario's debut season with RC Strasbourg was nothing short of remarkable. He quickly formed a potent partnership with his strike partner, Kevin Gameiro, and the duo terrorized defenses across Ligue 1. Mario's 10 goals in 27 appearances played a crucial role in Strasbourg's mid-table finish, a significant improvement from their previous season's relegation struggles.\nDocument 2: As the sun dipped behind the rolling hills of the Austrian countryside, Mario Haas pedaled his way through the winding roads, his legs pumping in rhythmic motion. The warm breeze carried the sweet scent of blooming wildflowers, and the sound of chirping birds accompanied him on his solo ride. It was a moment of tranquility, a brief respite from the grueling training sessions and high-stakes matches that had become his life as a professional footballer. As he approached the outskirts of the village, Mario noticed a group of cyclists clad in sleek, navy-blue jerseys with the Uniqa logo emblazoned on their backs. They were a familiar sight, and he couldn't help but feel a pang of nostalgia. He had often watched them train on these very roads, marveling at their endurance and precision. Mario slowed to a stop, watching as the cyclists expertly navigated a tight turn. One of them, a lanky rider with a mop of curly hair, caught his eye and flashed a friendly smile. It was Lukas, a young talent on the Team Uniqa roster. The two had struck up a conversation at a charity event a few months prior, bonding over their shared passion for sports and the great outdoors. As Mario prepared to continue his ride, Lukas pedaled over to him, sweat-drenched and grinning. \"Hey, Mario! Fancy seeing you here. What brings you out today?\" Mario shrugged, feeling a sense of camaraderie with the cyclist. \"Just getting in some extra cardio. You know how it is.\" Lukas nodded sympathetically. \"Tell me about it. We're gearing up for the Tour of Austria, and our coach is pushing us to the limit. But it's worth it – we're all about teamwork and supporting each other.\" Mario smiled, feeling a twinge of envy. He had always admired the sense of unity and purpose that seemed to define Team Uniqa. As a footballer, he was used to the intense rivalries and individualistic nature of his sport. As they chatted, a sleek, black team car pulled up behind the cyclists, the Uniqa logo emblazoned on its side. A tall, imposing figure emerged from the driver's seat, his eyes scanning the group before settling on Mario. \"Ah, Haas! What a surprise,\" he boomed, his voice like thunder on a summer day. \"I didn't know you were a fan of our team.\"\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
